WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
- Wow. 100,000 subscribers
on my Firm Learning Channel.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:07.110
This is really crazy.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.120
Thank you so much for every one of you

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.660
who was part of the journey.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.330
When I started out in January 2020,

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.000
I would never have thought,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.870
I would never even have dreamed,

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:19.570
that I would be able to
reach this milestone,

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.460
especially, not in this amount of time.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.100
So welcome to another coffee break

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.020
here on my channel, Firm Learning,

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:28.470
and the topic of today is Staffing.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:33.240
So the process how consultants
get on their new projects

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.210
can choose, or maybe not really
choose, their new projects.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:38.470
How this all works,

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.960
and I will share tips
in three areas with you.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.960
First of all, I will explain to you,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.040
hopefully also some non-obvious things,

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.810
why the staffing process
is really important

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:50.810
and really decisive
for your future career.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:54.560
Second, I will explain to you
how staffing processes work

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.420
in most large consulting firms.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.120
And third, I will give
you a couple of tips

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.510
what you can do to steer
the whole staffing process

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.050
in the right direction.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.160
So stay tuned.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.030
Now without further
ado, let's dive into it.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:09.510
So let's start by discussing

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.250
why the staffing process
is really important

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.520
and you should take it
really, really seriously.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.820
Because indeed when most firms
impose some kind of structure

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.330
and some kind of rules on you,

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.540
in most of the cases,
you do have flexibility

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.580
to steer the process in the direction

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:25.810
that you wanna have it to go.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.140
So first of all, maybe some
rather obvious reasons.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.800
If you do not take influence
on the staffing project,

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.660
you cannot really make sure
that you really do the projects

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.910
that you want to do.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.350
Maybe you're interested
in specific industries,

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.630
in specific functions, specific topics

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.140
that you wanna learn more about.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.730
Well, it is your
responsibility to make sure

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.790
that you steer the staffing
process in the direction

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:48.410
that indeed you have the opportunity

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.270
to do exactly this project.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:51.630
Next is the people.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.650
While most people you
work with in consulting

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.640
will be great, will be inspirational,

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.820
will be people you can
learn lots of things from,

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:01.250
as in all firms, there are differences.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.080
Probably some people will
be a bit more inspirational,

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.380
a bit more great to work with than others.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.310
Probably you will also,
on a personal level,

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:11.080
connect with some people
better than with others.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.910
So make sure that you really find

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:14.420
the people that you wanna work with.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.400
And that in the staffing process,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.750
you also get staffed on these projects

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.420
with these people that really
resonate well with you.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.920
Next, the reason why
staffing is important to you

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.440
is because you need to strategically think

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.060
about your exposure.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.510
If you always work together
with the same partner,

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.680
with the same one or two project leads

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.380
for an extended period of time,

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.940
and then your next
evaluation session is up

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.910
and maybe these people give
really great input on you.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.850
But think about it,
what will be more strong

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.710
to make your case to be a high performer,

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:44.730
to be a rockstar,

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.920
to potentially get that
fast track promotion?

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.210
Will it be strong if only
one person, one partner

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:52.670
cheers for you and tries
to make the case for you?

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:55.200
Or will it be stronger
if several partners,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.620
several senior employees of the company

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:58.740
will make the case for you?

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.400
So generally, you wanna make sure,

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.460
while it is great and helpful often

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.080
to really make bonds
with individual partners,

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.890
you wanna make sure that
you have a broad exposure,

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.870
that there's a certain set of people

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.977
who can all say positive things about you,

00:03:11.977 --> 00:03:13.830
who will cheer for you.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.760
This is the best way for
you to really make that case

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:18.180
of the strongest performance,

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.530
to make that case for the fast promotion.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.670
So if you are looking for your staff,

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:24.020
before your next staffing decision,

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:26.610
have your exposure matrix in mind as well,

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.690
to also look at this
from the strategic angle.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:31.170
So how does the staffing process works?

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.400
And of course, it's important
to mention in the beginning

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.170
that there are differences firm by firm.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.210
Some processes would rather tend towards

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:38.670
giving you as their consultant

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.310
lots of freedom in the choice.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:42.010
And other firms will rather push you

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:43.133
towards certain projects

00:03:43.133 --> 00:03:45.740
that they feel like
their most valuable ones.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.470
But in the end, with
pretty much all firms,

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.570
you do have significant freedom

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:52.070
to re-steer the whole process
in the right direction.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.450
And how it works is that
you usually can imagine

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:57.850
the staffing process to be
like a marketplace, okay?

00:03:57.850 --> 00:03:59.660
So on the one side of the market,

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:01.013
they're all the consultants.

00:04:01.013 --> 00:04:03.060
All the consultants that are now available

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.010
or just coming out of a
project that has now ended

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:08.010
and they're now looking for new projects.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.975
On the other side of this marketplace,

00:04:09.975 --> 00:04:12.170
they are the new projects
that are starting

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:14.020
and more precisely, the partners

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.480
that just sold the new
project that now need a team

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.070
to conduct this project.

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:20.680
And these are the two sides.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.620
Pretty much like a demand
side and a supply side

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:26.900
that need to come together to
then form the project team.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.400
So let's try to put
ourselves into the shoes

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.360
of these two market participants,

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:32.500
if you wanna frame it this way.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.130
So first, the consultants.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:34.963
What do they wanna do?

00:04:34.963 --> 00:04:36.930
Well, they of course
wanna have the project

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:38.350
that interests the most,

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.710
that is most closely
aligned with their goals,

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.460
where they can work
together with the people

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:43.947
that they wanna work together with,

00:04:43.947 --> 00:04:45.170
and so on and so forth.

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:47.220
So they will have very strong preferences

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.140
on what they wanna do.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:49.640
On the other side, the partners.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:50.710
What are they looking for?

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:51.860
Well, they wanna make sure

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:53.670
that the project that they're about to do

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:55.550
that they promised the client to deliver,

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:57.640
that they have the best team possible

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.270
to make this project a success.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:01.580
And this usually means that
they're looking for consultants

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:04.870
who already worked on
similar topics in the past.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:06.470
So this is like one very big requirement

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:07.470
that's usually there.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.430
But then the other second important one

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:11.670
is also, they're looking for consultants

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:13.200
that are just high performers.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.040
Consultants that have a
reputation of being rockstars,

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.950
of being those that just
really always deliver

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:20.880
or always doing a great job.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:22.290
So what does this mean to you?

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:23.123
Well, if you're a consultant

00:05:23.123 --> 00:05:25.010
and you wanna do a specific project,

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:28.260
if you have already done
related things in the past,

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:29.500
this will really help you then

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.530
to do more things in that direction.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.600
So usually just getting
the foot in the door

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:34.590
with a new type of project,

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:36.430
a new industry, a new function,

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:37.710
is the most important one.

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.400
After you've done that a couple of times

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.840
and made a name for yourself,

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.480
it will be much and much
easier going forward

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:45.070
to do more project in that direction.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:47.310
But then of course, your past performance

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:49.150
and your reputation in the firm

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:51.110
will be extremely important

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:53.870
in your ability to secure new projects

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:56.000
to really work on the
things that you wanna work.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.520
Another factor that plays into
this process is seasonality.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:02.610
So there are some times of the
year, typically the summer,

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:05.380
when not that many new
projects are starting.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.600
So this time, there are lots of associates

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.660
'on the beach' as it is called,

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:11.150
who are looking for new projects

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:13.430
but not that many projects that start.

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:16.240
So these times of the year
can be quite difficult

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.200
to really find the best projects

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.230
to exactly the projects that you wanna do.

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:21.290
But then on other times of the year,

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:23.756
usually also the start of a new year,

00:06:23.756 --> 00:06:25.970
there are lots of new projects going on.

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:27.610
Here there's really lots of projects,

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:31.240
lots of demand for
associates, for consultants

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:32.690
to start on a new project.

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:34.250
And in these times of the year

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:35.820
will always be much easier for you

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:37.360
to really get your choices

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:39.560
to projects that are aligned
with your preferences.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:40.890
So it is important to understand

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:43.720
this marketplace dynamic
of the staffing process.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:45.440
And make sure that you also network.

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.420
Build your network within the firm

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:48.840
because these are really the things

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.400
that then will help you in the long term

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.380
to really do the things that you wanna do

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:53.620
and follow your passions.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:56.650
So as promised, let me end
with a piece of advice.

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:59.140
Because there's one thing
that is very commonly done

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.540
by pretty much all more senior consultants

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:05.000
but then the junior ones often
don't really understand yet.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.390
And this is the importance
of doing due diligence

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:10.350
on your future project team.

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:11.830
So, let's imagine that you're in contact

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:13.600
with a new project that is starting

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.660
and it sounds quite
interesting, it sounds great,

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:17.740
it sounds like something
you would like to do.

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:19.650
And that's really
important, the next step,

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.180
that you make a due diligence on the team.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:24.430
And probably here the
most important person

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:26.250
to do the due diligence on

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.140
is the project lead.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:29.230
Because the project lead

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:31.590
will likely be the person
that determines most

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:35.180
how your daily experience
will be on the project.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:36.240
So what does this mean?

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.670
What exactly can you do?

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:39.700
Well, my experience is
that this is something

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:41.200
that is so common practice

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:42.850
that it's just absolutely okay

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:45.280
just to directly ask that project lead

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:46.527
if you talk to that person.

00:07:46.527 --> 00:07:48.207
"Can you name me a couple of references?"

00:07:48.207 --> 00:07:49.680
"Can you say me a couple of names

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:51.150
of people that you
worked with in the past?"

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:53.390
Usually then, these people
will be more than happy

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:54.620
to provide you a short list.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:56.027
And then you should reach
out to these people,

00:07:56.027 --> 00:07:59.080
either with a short email
or ideally on the phone

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:00.167
calling them up saying,

00:08:00.167 --> 00:08:03.090
"Hey, I saw that you worked
with this person in the past,

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:05.500
can you tell me a couple
of things about the person?

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:07.600
How was it like to work with that person?"

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:09.120
Then of course if the project

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.620
is already ongoing for quite some time,

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:13.927
also ask for the names of the people

00:08:13.927 --> 00:08:16.770
who work currently as
consultants on the project

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:18.350
or maybe worked on the project

00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:20.270
a couple of weeks, a couple of months ago.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:21.430
This is also great way

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:23.890
of getting a more independent opinion

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:25.230
or more independent impression

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:27.580
of what it's really like to
work there at the moment.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:28.970
Because often the situation,

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:31.960
the experience is not only
determined by your own team

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:34.040
but also how the client team is set up.

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:35.990
So how are the clients like

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:38.360
that you would interact
with on a daily basis.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:40.010
Are these people fun to work with?

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:41.640
Are they great to interact with?

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:42.600
As you can imagine,

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:45.820
there are also huge differences
on the client's side

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:48.710
that can also significantly
shape your experience.

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:50.130
My additional point here would be

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:53.680
to always be very deliberate
on how you ask the questions

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:55.300
if you conduct such due diligence.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.260
Because it is the most cases,

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:59.600
other consultants will be quite hesitant

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:01.350
to not blatantly say

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.930
that a certain person was
terrible to work with,

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:05.440
that it was super difficult,

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:07.300
super bad, the whole experience.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:09.610
So often, unless you really
know already these people,

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:11.440
the feedback will be much more nuanced.

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:13.787
So one question that I
always like to ask is,

00:09:13.787 --> 00:09:16.170
"Okay, on a scale from 1 to 10,

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.830
how much did you enjoy working
with that project lead?"

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:20.000
And here, my experience was,

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:22.210
if people really loved that person,

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:23.043
they will tell you,

00:09:23.043 --> 00:09:25.870
"Well, on a scale from
1 to 10, it's a hundred.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:28.680
It's a 20. It's 50. It's like much more.

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:30.870
It's 10 plus to work with that person."

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:32.657
But then if you get a response of like,

00:09:32.657 --> 00:09:36.010
"Ah okay, it was a 5 or a 6
to work with that person."

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:37.190
That probably would be something

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:38.920
where you would wanna dig in to that,

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:40.290
be a bit more careful.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:41.947
Then of course, you can also ask like,

00:09:41.947 --> 00:09:44.920
"If you would need to name one thing

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:46.130
that was really not great,

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:47.240
that was really something

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:49.520
where this project lead
needs to improve on,

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:50.830
what would that be?"

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:52.580
But if you're really
like pressing the person

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:54.410
to name one or two or three things,

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:55.850
what are these people then telling you?

00:09:55.850 --> 00:09:57.620
So these are really the type of questions

00:09:57.620 --> 00:09:58.770
that you wanna go for.

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.590
Don't go with too soft,
too open questions.

00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:04.610
Because these then will likely
give you too soft answers

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:07.070
that are not really insightful
for you for your decision.

